From: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: say why GDM3/GDM4 QoS is refused on a WAN port

Adding an offloaded HTB qdisc to a LAN port backed by GDM3 or GDM4 fails
with -EBUSY when GDM2 already carries the WAN role.  That is structural,
not a transient conflict: the GDM3/GDM4 offload path loops GDM2 back into
the target port and forwards the shaped traffic from there, so it needs
GDM2 to itself, and on boards where GDM2 is the WAN port there is no
second path to shape through.

The existing message ("QoS configured for WAN device") describes this as
if some configuration had already claimed the resource, which sends people
looking for a qdisc that is not there.  Report the two cases separately:
a qdisc actually present on the WAN device, which the user can tear down,
and GDM2 being the WAN port, which is a permanent property of the board.

No behaviour change; the same requests are refused, with a message that
matches what the hardware is doing.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_eth.c
@@ -3234,10 +3234,23 @@
 
 	wan_dev = airoha_get_wan_gdm_dev(eth);
 	if (wan_dev) {
-		if (test_bit(AIROHA_DEV_F_TX_QOS, &wan_dev->flags) ||
-		    wan_dev->port->id == AIROHA_GDM2_IDX) {
+		if (test_bit(AIROHA_DEV_F_TX_QOS, &wan_dev->flags)) {
 			NL_SET_ERR_MSG_MOD(extack,
-					   "QoS configured for WAN device");
+					   "QoS already configured for WAN device");
+			err = -EBUSY;
+			goto error_unlock;
+		}
+
+		if (wan_dev->port->id == AIROHA_GDM2_IDX) {
+			/* GDM3/GDM4 QoS works by looping GDM2 back into the
+			 * target port and forwarding the shaped traffic from
+			 * there, so it needs GDM2 to itself. On a board where
+			 * GDM2 carries the WAN role there is no second path
+			 * to shape through, which makes HW QoS on GDM3/GDM4
+			 * and a WAN port mutually exclusive.
+			 */
+			NL_SET_ERR_MSG_MOD(extack,
+					   "GDM2 carries the WAN port and is needed for the GDM3/GDM4 QoS loopback");
 			err = -EBUSY;
 			goto error_unlock;
 		}
